## HTML classes, IDs, and iframes, focusing on their use cases, best practices, and modern development perspectives. 

Key Concepts and Explanations

# Classes and IDs in HTML
- Classes and IDs are primarily used to select and style HTML elements using CSS.
- Classes can be assigned to multiple elements, allowing reusable styles and group targeting, Same class can be used on many elements.
### 🧾 Example
<p class="red">This is paragraph 1</p>
<p class="red">This is paragraph 2</p>

- IDs must be unique within a webpage and are intended for reliable, unique element targeting.
- The primary role of classes and IDs is for element selection and styling; however, they can also be used by frameworks to maintain references in memory for dynamic manipulation.


## CSS selectors:
- Classes are selected using a dot (.) prefix, e.g., .red.

# CSS
.red {
  background-color: red;
}

- IDs are selected using a hash (#) prefix, e.g., #green. (Used for unique elements, Must be used only once per page)

# HTML
<h1 id="green">Heading</h1>

# CSS
#green {
  background-color: green;
}

- The video demonstrates a simple webpage with paragraphs and headings, applying class .red and ID green to elements to show background color changes.

# Class: Usage Can be reused on multiple elements, . classname selector in CSS (keypoint)

# ID: Usage Should be unique per page, #idname selector in CSS; unique use (keypoints)

## Purpose
- Used for styling elements with CSS
- Helps in targeting elements easily

## Used in frameworks for:
- DOM manipulation
- Dynamic updates

- Although HTML does not strictly enforce uniqueness of IDs, **good practice demands unique IDs per page**.
- Modern frameworks (e.g., React, Angular) **automatically generate unique IDs** and mostly use classes for styling and targeting.
- The video inspects LinkedIn’s DOM, showing **automatically generated unique IDs** like `amber241`, `amber242`, etc., used internally by frameworks for element referencing.
- The **market convention currently favors using multiple classes over many IDs**, but the uniqueness of IDs is still important for specific targeting.

# Examples Combined

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    .red {
      background-color: red;
    }

    #green {
      background-color: green;
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <p class="red">This is a red paragraph</p>
  <h1 id="green">This is a green heading</h1>
